# Code generation standards

## TypeScript

- Strict mode is on. Never use `any`; use `unknown` + narrowing, or define an explicit type.
- Prefer `interface` for public/exported shapes, `type` for unions and utility types.
- Exhaustive `switch` on discriminated unions — let unused branches fail the build, don't add `default:` just to silence the check.
- Name types in `PascalCase`, values in `camelCase`, enum‑like constants in `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE`.

## React

- Functional components only. No class components.
- One component per file, default export matches the file name. Helpers used only by that component live in the same file.
- Props typed as `interface ComponentNameProps`. Do not use `React.FC`.
- Keep components under ~200 lines. If a page grows, extract subcomponents into `src/components/`.
- Derive state from props; lift state up only when two siblings need it.
- Use existing providers (`CartProvider`, `LanguageProvider`) — do not add parallel global state.

## Imports

- Order: node builtins → third‑party → absolute `src/…` → relative. ESLint/Prettier handle spacing.
- No circular imports. If you need to break one, prefer moving shared types to `src/types/`.
- Use named exports for utilities and contexts; default exports for components and pages only.

## Assets

- Any path under `/products`, `/slider`, `/videos` must go through `getAssetPath()` from `src/utils/images.ts`. Never hard‑code these paths in JSX or data.
- Images need `alt` text. Decorative images use `alt=""` plus `aria-hidden="true"`.
- Videos need `aria-label` and should default to muted + playsInline.

## Accessibility

- Every interactive element needs an accessible name (`aria-label` for icon‑only buttons).
- Keyboard navigation must work for all interactive elements — don't attach click handlers to `<div>`.
- Prefer semantic elements (`<button>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<header>`) over styled `<div>`s.

## Comments

- Write code that doesn't need a comment first. Only comment to explain *why* something non‑obvious was done (perf constraint, browser bug, business rule).
- No narrative comments ("// fetch the user"). No emoji comments.

## Forbidden

- `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error` without a linked issue and a timeline to remove.
- `eslint-disable` without a single‑line explanation.
- `console.log` in committed code. Use a proper error surface or delete the debug log.
- New runtime dependencies without checking bundle size impact; this is a small SPA.

## If unsure about a library API

Use Context7 — see `context7.mdc`.
